eNews","byline":"Mark Schlabach","source":"ESPN"},{"headline":"Breaking down the Heisman race: How Week 10 could change everything","description":"Pamela Maldonado breaks down where things stand on the Heisman odds board with a new favorite taking over.","published":"2026-11-04T02:23:18.000+00:00","url":"https://www.espn.com/espn/betting/story/_/id/50360375/college-football-heisman-2026-odds-favorites-picks-week-10","type":"Story","byline":"Pamela Maldonado","source":"ESPN"},{"headline":"Wake Forest's post-blowout jab at SMU tops college football Week 11 trolls","description":"Wake Forest dominated SMU on the gridiron Saturday -- and added insult to injury after the game.","published":"2026-11-14T23:36:57.000+00:00","url":"https://www.espn.com/college-football/story/_/id/50481104/college-football-trolls-week-11","type":"Story","byline":"ESPN staff","source":"ESPN"},{"headline":"Watch best moments and top plays live from Week 11","published":"2026-11-14T23:16:30.000+00:00","url":"https://www.espn.com/college-football/story/_/id/50474063/week-11-live-highlights","type":"Story","byline":"ESPN staff","source":"ESPN"},{"headline":"Darian Mensah dices up ex-team Duke as Miami rebounds from first loss","description":"Miami quarterback Darian Mensah diced up his former team Saturday, finishing with 382 yards and a touchdown in the 36-25 victory over Duke.","published":"2026-11-14T23:57:48.000+00:00","url":"https://www.espn.com/college-football/story/_/id/50484151/darian-mensah-dices-ex-team-duke-miami-rebounds-first-loss","type":"HeadlineNews","byline":"Andrea Adelson","source":"ESPN"},{"headline":"Wake Forest strengthens CFP case with 'statement' win at SMU","description":"No. 13 Wake Forest made a statement to the College Football Playoff selection committee with a 49-7 blowout of SMU on Saturday.","published":"2026-11-14T22:49:36.000+00:00","url":"https://www.espn.com/college-football/story/_/id/50483729/wake-forest-strengthens-cfp-case-statement-win-smu","type":"HeadlineNews","source":"ESPN"},{"headline":"Vols lose top receiver Matthews to knee injury vs. Texas A&M","description":"Tennessee leading receiver Mike Matthews suffered a right knee injury in the second quarter of Saturday's game at Texas A&M.","published":"2026-11-14T23:16:10.000+00:00","url":"https://www.espn.com/college-football/story/_/id/50483677/vols-lose-top-receiver-matthews-knee-injury-vs-texas-am","type":"HeadlineNews","byline":"Mark Schlabach","source":"ESPN"},{"headline":"Georgia commit, DL Marcellus Young Casario died after 'sudden medical emergency'","description":"Marcellus Young Casario, a Georgia football commit, died Friday after experiencing a \"sudden medical emergency,\" according to his high school.","published":"2026-11-14T20:36:58.000+00:00","url":"https://www.espn.com/college-football/story/_/id/50482223/georgia-football-commit-marcellus-young-casario-dies-suddenly","type":"HeadlineNews","byline":"Eli Lederman","source":"ESPN"},{"headline":"Oklahoma AD rips Ole Miss, SEC; Rebels defend travel effort","description":"Oklahoma AD Roger Denny ripped the SEC and Ole Miss for the \"inexplicable\" decision to move its game to Sunday, while the Rebels defended their travel efforts.","published":"2026-11-14T23:54:22.000+00:00","url":"https://www.espn.com/college-football/story/_/id/50482206/oklahoma-ad-infuriated-sec-ole-miss-sunday-game","type":"HeadlineNews","byline":"Max Olson","source":"ESPN"},{"headline":"Clemson QB Reynolds to have MRI on knee; backup Vizzina (ankle) also hurt","description":"Clemson quarterback Tait Reynolds is having an MRI on his injured knee, and backup Christopher Vizzina was sent to the hospital to evaluate his ankle.","published":"2026-11-14T23:52:45.000+00:00","url":"https://www.espn.com/college-football/story/_/id/50482105/clemson-qb-tait-reynolds-suffers-leg-injury-slide","type":"HeadlineNews","byline":"Andrea Adelson","source":"ESPN"}],"links":{"espn":"https://www.espn.com/college-football/player/_/id/4911529/trinidad-chambliss","wikipedia":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trinidad_Chambliss"},"pick":{"pick":17,"round":1,"team":"SEA","provisional":false},"wiki":{"rules":4,"title":"Trinidad Chambliss","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trinidad_Chambliss","description":"American football player (born 2002)","touched":"2026-11-14T07:37:14Z","intro":"Trinidad Jay Chambliss (; born August 24, 2002) is an American college football quarterback for the Ole Miss Rebels. He previously played for the Ferris State Bulldogs, leading the 2024 Bulldogs to the Division II national championship.","sections":[{"key":"early","title":"Early life","text":"Chambliss attended Forest Hills Northern High School in Grand Rapids, Michigan. As a senior, he threw for 1,610 yards and 17 touchdowns. After graduating high school, he committed to play college football at Ferris State University."},{"key":"college","title":"College career","text":"FERRIS STATE\nAs a junior in 2024, Chambliss threw for 2,925 yards and 26 touchdowns and rushed for 1,019 yards and 25 touchdowns, leading Ferris State to a Division II national championship. In the national championship game against Valdosta State, he combined for five touchdowns in the 49–14 victory. As a result of his play, Chambliss was named the Great Lakes Intercollegiate Athletic Conference Player of the Year and a finalist for the Harlon Hill Trophy. Following the conclusion of the season, he entered the transfer portal.\n\nOLE MISS\nIn April 2025, Chambliss announced his decision to transfer to the University of Mississippi to play for the Ole Miss Rebels. He entered the season as the backup to Austin Simmons. In the season opener against Georgia State, he completed four passes for 59 yards and a touchdown. Following an injury to Simmons, Chambliss was named the Rebels' starting quarterback against Arkansas. In his first start for Ole Miss, he combined for 415 total yards and three touchdowns in a 41–35 triumph over Arkansas. In the Egg Bowl against Mississippi State, Chambliss completed 23 passes for 359 yards and four touchdowns, leading the Rebels to a 38–19 victory and clinching a spot in the College Football Playoff. At the conclusion of the regular season, he was named the SEC Newcomer of the Year and recipient of the Conerly Trophy. In the first round of the playoffs against Tulane, Chambliss threw for 282 yards and combined for three total touchdowns in a win. In the 2026 Sugar Bowl, he completed 30 passes for a season-high 362 yards and two touchdowns, leading Ole Miss to a 39–34 upset victory in a rematch against Georgia; he was named the game's offensive MVP for his efforts.\nIn November 2025, Ole Miss filed a waiver request with the NCAA to allow Chambliss a sixth year of eligibility, as he did not play during his sophomore season at Ferris State in 2022 due to persistent respiratory issues. On January 9, 2026, the NCAA denied the request, ending his college eligibility. Ole Miss filed an appeal with the NCAA. On January 16, lawyers representing Chambliss sued the NCAA in Mississippi state court, seeking preliminary and permanent injunctions that would allow him to play an additional season. On February 5, 2026, Chambliss' appeal was rejected again. Following another appeal, a judge granted Chambliss an injunction on February 12, 2026, allowing him a sixth year of college eligibility.\nChambliss returned to Ole Miss in 2026 rather than enter the 2026 NFL draft. In the season opener, the Music City Kickoff against No. 24 Louisville, he threw for 336 yards, three touchdowns, and an interception while rushing for 60 yards and a touchdown. Against his former head coach Lane Kiffin and No. 7 LSU, Chambliss led the Rebels to a 32–24 victory, completing 33 passes for 363 yards and accounting for three touchdowns."}]}}